.TH OC-BOX 1 "" "oc-box" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
.PP
oc\-box \- persistent rootless Podman environments for mainline OpenCode.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box [start] [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [START\-OPTIONS]
oc\-box stop [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box sync [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box reset [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [RESET\-OPTIONS]
oc\-box repair [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box terminal [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box purge [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [\-\-delete\-sessions | \-\-all] [\-y]
oc\-box list
oc\-box distros
oc\-box doctor [\-\-json] [\-\-feature FEATURE ...]
oc\-box \-\-status [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [\-\-json]
oc\-box status [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [\-\-json]
oc\-box upgrade [\-\-check | \-\-auto\-update on|off]
oc\-box secrets init|list|passwd
oc\-box secrets set|remove NAME
oc\-box credentials [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [CREDENTIAL\-OPTIONS]
oc\-box backup [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] \-\-output FILE [\-\-portable] [\-\-recipient AGE_KEY ...]
oc\-box backup\-info FILE [\-\-identity FILE ...]
oc\-box restore FILE \-\-project NEW_PATH [\-\-identity FILE ...]
oc\-box \-\-about
oc\-box \-\-version
oc\-box \-h | \-\-help
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The command defaults to \fBstart\fR\&. Place options before or after the command. Run the launcher on the host as your regular user, without sudo. It manages rootless Podman; container root is mapped to the unprivileged host user.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
oc\-box starts unmodified OpenCode and its local MCP servers, plugins, formatters, language servers, shell, and file tools together inside a persistent development container. It associates one environment with one canonical project directory. The entire project, including hidden files, Git history, untracked files, and unfinished edits, is mounted writable at the same absolute path.
.PP
The container HOME has the host user's home pathname but uses a private named volume. It is not a mount of the host home. The live OpenCode database, session IDs, snapshots, caches, and home\-installed dependencies persist in this volume. Packages installed elsewhere in the OS persist in the container's writable layer until that container is reset or removed.
.PP
On normal exit, oc\-box exports conversation history to separate archive sessions in the normal host OpenCode database, then stops the container. Launching oc\-box again resumes the original container session. Running processes do not survive a stop. Host OpenCode and its configuration are unchanged. Build mode permits all OpenCode tool permissions inside oc\-box; Plan mode and subagents retain their own rules.
.PP
The default resource limits are 4 CPU cores, 8 GiB RAM, and 2048 processes. These are ceilings, not reservations: resources are consumed dynamically up to those limits, and OpenCode itself has no fixed 4/8 GiB minimum requirement. There is no disk quota. Containers share the host kernel and can reach network services, including configured host services at \fBhost.containers.internal\fR\&. The project is intentionally writable and can be deleted by container commands. Additional Podman options and devices explicitly extend these defaults.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS doctor
.PP
Report prerequisites by feature, missing commands, and installation commands for the OS running doctor. It is read\-only: no packages installed, network requests, Podman operations, workspace/config changes, or secret prompts. Run on the host for host advice. Inside oc\-box it explicitly reports \fBcurrent\-container\fR; this cannot inspect host packages. A workspace's selected distro does not determine host package guidance. Unknown hosts list required providers without guessing a package manager from the default workspace image or executing os\-release text.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box doctor
oc\-box doctor \-\-feature backup \-\-feature vault
oc\-box doctor \-\-feature backup\-info \-\-json
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Feature selectors: \fBlaunch\fR, \fBmaintenance\fR, \fBhistory\fR, \fBbackup\fR, \fBbackup\-info\fR, \fBrestore\fR, \fBvault\fR, \fBgithub\-upgrade\fR, \fBbootstrap\fR, \fBstorage\fR, \fBoverlay\fR, \fBmanuals\fR, and \fBdocs\-build\fR\&. Without selectors all are reported, but exit status checks only core \fBlaunch\fR prerequisites. With selectors, exit 0 means every selected feature's prerequisites are present; exit 1 means at least one is missing or incompatible. JSON schema version 1 includes scope, detected distro, feature availability, dependency details, missing providers, package names, installation commands, and notes. Missing optional features never disable unrelated commands. Feature commands check their prerequisites before secret prompts or destructive workspace changes and include OS\-specific fixes.
.PP
This checks installed prerequisites, not full runtime certification. Podman still checks rootless mode, subordinate IDs, controller delegation, and mounts; interactive commands check terminal requirements. Command presence does not establish a compatible version or validate every backend/driver configuration. Normal OpenCode launch verifies its host/container versions separately.
.SS start
.PP
Create or resume an environment. This is the default when no command is given. The project directory must already exist and be dedicated to the project. Without a selector, use the current directory. With \fB\-\-workspace\fR, use the registered directory; a workspace ID takes precedence if both selectors are provided. Do not combine selectors when a single selector will do.
.PP
On first launch, detect the host distro, create a labeled container and private home volume, install baseline tools, install the exact host OpenCode release, copy configuration, resolve plugin dependencies, and start a conversation. Afterward, start the existing environment and resume its latest recorded root conversation. \fB\-\-new\fR starts another conversation in the same environment.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
mkdir \-p ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box start \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-new
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Cold startup includes image/package/plugin downloads; timed stage messages appear every five seconds. A loading line covers blank TUI startup frames and disappears when OpenCode draws text. Warm launches retain compatible caches. The container OpenCode version is verified against the host on every launch; exact upgrades and downgrades are supported, with no fallback to another release.
.SS stop
.PP
Stop a registered container without deleting the project, container OS, private home, session IDs, or metadata. Already stopped or missing containers need no start. This command can run from a second host terminal while OpenCode is active. Its processes stop, and the active client may disconnect or report tool errors. Exit that launcher before starting another instance or resetting the workspace. Use \fBsync\fR afterward if a final history export was missed.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box stop \-\-project ~/devel/demo
.fam T
.fi
.SS sync
.PP
Refresh host conversation archives without opening the TUI. Requires an existing workspace and an intact project directory. It starts or recreates the container if needed, prepares its runtime/configuration, discovers saved root sessions, exports them, and imports separate namespaced archive copies into the host database. It stops the container afterward, even if it had been running.
.PP
An active oc\-box launcher holds a workspace lock, so exit it before syncing. Use this after an OpenCode crash, missed exit\-time sync, or host restart. Sync failure preserves container data; retry after resolving the error.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box sync \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box sync \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID
.fam T
.fi
.SS terminal
.PP
Open an interactive maintenance shell in an existing workspace container instead of starting OpenCode. Select the current project, \fB\-\-project PATH\fR, or \fB\-\-workspace ID\fR\&. Only these selectors and help are accepted. Run from a real host terminal with TTY input/output; piped or redirected input/output is refused.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box terminal
oc\-box terminal \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box terminal \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Exit the active OpenCode launcher first: maintenance holds the same exclusive workspace lock as other lifecycle operations. This prevents a concurrent reset, purge, or normal launch. It verifies the original project identity, matching marker, container ownership, and project mount. It never creates a new workspace or container, reinstalls tools, refreshes configuration/auth, calls OpenCode, or synchronizes history. If the container is missing, use a normal launch to recreate it explicitly. A partially initialized but existing container can be entered for debugging without rerunning baseline downloads.
.PP
If the container is stopped, terminal starts it and stops it again on shell exit (including error/interrupt cleanup). If already running without an active launcher, it is left running. A paused container must be unpaused by the host operator first. Type \fBexit\fR or press Ctrl\-D to leave. Ctrl\-C interrupts shell commands; terminal sizing, input, and resize events are relayed normally. The command returns the maintenance shell/Podman exit code. The session IDs, source files, and private home are not changed by the launcher; your own shell commands can change them. Packages installed in the OS layer survive stop/start but not reset, just as when the AI installs them.
.PP
The shell runs with the container's existing user, devices, resource limits, mounts, and privileges; terminal adds no mounts or capabilities. Container root is still rootless on the host. It opens Bash with \fB\-\-noprofile \-\-norc \-i\fR, or \fB/\:bin/\:sh \-i\fR when Bash is unavailable. Shell startup files are not loaded, and history persistence is disabled (\fBHISTFILE=/\:dev/\:null\fR, history sizes zero).
.PP
Unlike a normal OpenCode launch, terminal does not forward API keys, tokens, custom \fBpass_env\fR, proxy variables, agent sockets, or shell/loader hooks. The Podman client receives only allowlisted host runtime/storage discovery settings; its child shell starts with \fBenv \-i\fR and a small fixed environment: the private HOME, project PWD, container PATH, validated TERM, Bash SHELL, \fBC.UTF\-8\fR locale, container marker, and history controls. Credentials are not put in arguments, printed, or copied into the container by this command.
.PP
This is maintenance access to the existing environment, not a second security boundary inside it. Previously copied auth/config files, secrets deliberately written to disk, and environments of other processes already in the container remain accessible under normal container permissions. To inspect environment variables, avoid posting unredacted output from unrelated running processes. Starting an existing container also resumes its configured entrypoint unchanged; terminal does not change create\-time environment or undo earlier exposure.
.PP
For example, after mapping an NPU in an earlier reset:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box terminal \-\-project ~/devel/npu
ls \-l /dev/accel/accel0
prlimit \-\-pid $$ \-\-memlock
xrt\-smi examine
exit
.fam T
.fi
.SS reset
.PP
Recreate the environment's container OS, then launch OpenCode and resume work. Exit the active workspace launcher first. Reset removes the owned container and owned networks while retaining the private home/session volume and any other workspace\-owned volumes, project, Git history, unfinished edits, session IDs, and resume metadata. Installed system packages and files outside the project/private home must be recreated; running processes stop. Baseline tools and OpenCode are reinstalled automatically.
.PP
Use reset to change distro, CPU/RAM/process limits, devices, or extra Podman settings. These are not live updates in oc\-box, even though Podman itself can update some limits. No purge is required. Native binaries cached in the home may need reinstalling when switching distributions, especially to/from Alpine.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-cpus 8 \-\-memory 16g
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro ubuntu
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0
.fam T
.fi
.SS repair
.PP
Explicitly accept the current directory identity for an existing workspace at its original canonical path. Use this after a filesystem identity change or a deliberate project restore when you want to retain the existing environment. Only run it after checking that the directory is the project you intend to use. The host registry must still exist. A matching regular \fB\&.oc\-box\-id\fR is retained; if the marker is missing, repair can restore it from that registry. Symlink, invalid, or another workspace's markers are never overwritten. Repair cannot relocate a workspace or reconstruct missing registry/session data.
.PP
Ordinary device\-number reassignment after reboot does not need repair once a stable filesystem/handle baseline has been recorded. Old registrations that already mismatch and lack that baseline still need one explicit repair; matching legacy registrations are upgraded by the next normal launch.
.PP
Exit the active launcher and stop its container first. Repair takes the workspace lock, checks container ownership, and refuses a running container. It updates the registry's directory device/inode and supported filesystem\-handle baseline, and restores a missing marker; project files, container OS, private home, sessions, and saved settings are retained. It does not start or reset the container. An already matching identity needs no change.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box repair \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-resume
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Accepts only \fB\-\-project\fR or \fB\-\-workspace\fR selectors, besides help. With neither, use the current directory. If a missing marker has exactly one registered workspace for this path, \fB\-\-project\fR selects it. If several records reference the path, repair refuses to choose: inspect \fB\-\-status\fR and supply \fB\-\-workspace ID\fR explicitly. With \fB\-\-workspace\fR, check the registered path and restore its missing marker rather than using the current directory.
.SS purge
.PP
Remove this workspace's labeled containers, owned volumes, owned networks, resume metadata, private recovery exports, and matching project marker. Exit its active launcher first. Shared base\-image caches and unrelated Podman resources are retained; cleanup does not use a global prune.
.PP
Default purge retains project files and host archive conversations. It attempts a final history sync while the project and container exist. If that sync fails, the private volume is retained for recovery. If the project was already deleted, there is no final sync; the last host archives remain.
.PP
Before removing the active registry, plain purge also retains its verified archive JSON files and minimal workspace/project provenance privately under \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box/\:retired/\:ID/\:\fR (or the configured state root). These are not active workspaces and do not appear in list/status. They allow a host archive that still names the old controller directory to be imported later. They may contain private conversation content, just like host archives. The explicit \fB\-\-delete\-sessions\fR and \fB\-\-all\fR variants do not create this recovery copy.
.PP
\fB\-\-delete\-sessions\fR additionally deletes the recorded host archive copies, not an original host conversation imported into oc\-box. \fB\-\-all\fR additionally deletes the entire project directory. Project deletion requires the original directory identity and matching marker; if either belongs to another workspace, \fB\-\-all\fR is rejected before deleting any history or resources. Extra externally supplied mounts/volumes are not owned resources unless they bear this workspace's ownership label.
.PP
By default, enter the first eight characters of the workspace ID to confirm. \fB\-y\fR or \fB\-\-yes\fR skips this confirmation. These deletion flags apply only to purge.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-delete\-sessions
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/disposable \-\-all
oc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-all \-\-yes
.fam T
.fi
.SS list
.PP
List all registered workspaces with aligned headers: WORKSPACE ID, DISTRO, PROJECT, STATUS, and SESSION ID. No Podman invocation or container start is needed. Missing project directories remain listed so they can be purged by ID. STATUS describes directory identity/marker health, not container running state: \fBpresent\fR, \fBidentity changed\fR, \fBmarker missing\fR, \fBmarker mismatch\fR, \fBinvalid marker\fR, \fBmissing directory\fR, \fBreplaced directory\fR (not a regular directory), or \fBinaccessible\fR\&. Device\-only changes with a matching recorded filesystem ID, persistent handle, and inode remain \fBpresent\fR; status does not rewrite the registry. Unverified identity changes are not assumed to mean replacement: legacy records may lack a stable baseline. A marker mismatch means another workspace is selected for that path. Recovery/cleanup hints go to stderr and never delete or repair records automatically.
.PP
SESSION ID is the original container session used for resume, or \fBnone\fR\&. Local metadata is refreshed at startup/sync; a new conversation in a running client may not be reflected until the next sync or launch. With no workspaces, the headers are still printed. The list is global, not filtered by selectors; use \fB\-\-status\fR for a selected environment and machine\-readable output.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
.fam T
.fi
.SS distros
.PP
List supported distro names, image references, and the detected host default. No Podman invocation is required. This is a fixed supported image catalog; there is no custom\-image option. Selection is stored per workspace.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box distros
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro fedora
.fam T
.fi
.SS status
.PP
Alias of \fB\-\-status\fR\&. Report all registered environments unless \fB\-\-project\fR or \fB\-\-workspace\fR selects one. A selector must refer to an existing workspace. Status is read\-only and can run while OpenCode is active; it does not start, stop, or execute commands inside managed containers. Use \fB\-\-json\fR for numeric, machine\-readable output. See Resource reports for metric interpretation.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box status
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-json
.fam T
.fi
.SS upgrade
.PP
Check and update the installed oc\-box launcher/modules and prebuilt manuals from the fixed public GitHub repository, branch \fBmaster\fR\&. Run on the host as your regular user; no sudo or GitHub token is needed. This command works without Podman or a registered workspace. It does not update host OpenCode, modify a source checkout, reset a container, or touch project/session data.
.PP
This command's GitHub update path applies only to curl/source\-installed copies. Python\-package installations detect their packaged distribution marker and print \fBpipx upgrade oc\-box\fR, \fBuv tool upgrade oc\-box\fR, or the current interpreter's pip upgrade command instead, without downloading or installing anything. They reject \fB\-\-auto\-update on\fR because a package manager owns those files; normal launches skip GitHub automatic updates even if a shared host config enables them. \fB\-\-auto\-update off\fR on a package\-managed copy prints that package guidance and does not modify the GitHub installation's shared setting. Both channels use the same runtime/workspace format and remain supported in parallel.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box upgrade \-\-check
oc\-box upgrade
oc\-box upgrade \-\-auto\-update on
oc\-box upgrade \-\-auto\-update off
.fam T
.fi
.PP
\fB\-\-check\fR compares the installed commit with GitHub and reports whether it differs, without installing. A normal upgrade clones the full public branch history into a temporary directory, pins the checked commit, verifies source identity, and runs its staged user\-local installer. An identical clean build does not download the full history again. A locally installed build whose commit count is ahead of the published branch is retained rather than downgraded. Source\-checkout updates remain a separate \fBgit pull\fR/review task: upgrade always targets the installed user\-local copy. A changed published commit at the same count can replace a different installed build.
.PP
The installer stages a complete release under \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:oc\-box/\:releases/\:\fR, including license and manuals, before switching the launcher. New processes use the new version; active launchers keep their original release path and lazy\-load its original modules. Old release directories are retained and have no automatic garbage collection. \fBcurrent\fR and compatible top\-level installed paths point to the activated release. An older flat installation is migrated; its old manuals are preserved as \fBlegacy\-docs\fR\&. No workspaces or credentials are migrated/deleted.
.PP
Automatic updates are off by default. \fB\-\-auto\-update on\fR saves \fBauto_update: true\fR in the existing host oc\-box configuration, retaining settings such as \fBpass_env\fR\&. Normal installed \fBstart\fR launches check before Podman/workspace setup and, when updated, continue with the same arguments. No background daemon, active\-session update, or automatic check for list/status/terminal/reset/purge is introduced. Direct source\-checkout launches skip automatic updates. A concurrent check does not block another normal launcher. Network/download failures are reported and the normal launch continues with the installed version; explicit upgrade failures return nonzero. After enabling automatic updates you are trusting future code published to this development branch, not signed release artifacts.
.PP
Fetch/checkout/install subprocesses have a clean, positive\-allowlisted environment. Global/system Git config, helpers, hooks, external protocols, provider tokens, SSH agents, and user proxy variables are not used by the public download. HTTPS is required; shell commands or a custom upstream URL are not accepted. Use manual source installation for a reviewed alternate deployment. Updates inside a container are refused because its private HOME is not the host installation. Git is required for public updates; build tools are not.
.SS secrets
.PP
Manage an optional encrypted credential vault on the host. This command never starts Podman or copies secrets into the development environment. The existing env\-file workflow is unchanged unless you select vault credentials explicitly. Use the literal \fBsecrets\fR command first, followed by one action and, for set or remove, an uppercase environment name. Values and passphrases are accepted only through masked controlling\-terminal prompts, never argv, stdin, or environment password settings. Host\-only; do not run it inside a development container.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box secrets init
oc\-box secrets set PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN
oc\-box secrets set BRAVE_API_KEY
oc\-box secrets list
oc\-box secrets remove OLD_TOKEN
oc\-box secrets passwd
# Override the saved minimum for a new/changed passphrase:
oc\-box secrets passwd \-\-min\-passphrase\-length 8
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Actions: \fBinit\fR creates an empty vault after entering/confirming a passphrase; it never overwrites an existing vault. \fBset NAME\fR unlocks, then enters/confirms the secret value and creates or replaces that entry. \fBlist\fR unlocks and prints only sorted names, never values. \fBremove NAME\fR unlocks and deletes the named entry; a missing entry causes a clear error without changes. \fBpasswd\fR unlocks with the old passphrase and asks for a new confirmed passphrase. No \fBget\fR, plaintext dump, insecure batch password option, or automated env\-file import is provided. \fBoc\-box secrets \-\-help\fR describes the action syntax.
.PP
Input feedback shows a mask and character count for typing and pasting. Secret value and confirmation prompts start masked and offer **Ctrl+R** to toggle visible/masked input on the fly. Passphrase creation, confirmation, and unlock prompts always stay masked: Ctrl+R does not reveal or modify passphrases. Reveal state never carries to the next prompt. There is no flag or setting that makes credentials visible by default.
.PP
Editing keys: Left/Right move, Home/End (or Ctrl+A/Ctrl+E) select the ends, Backspace/Delete remove a character, Ctrl+U clears the entire value, Ctrl+W removes the preceding word, and Ctrl+K clears from the cursor to the end. Enter accepts; Ctrl+C, Ctrl+D, or Ctrl+Z cancels without changing the vault. Ctrl+Z cancels rather than suspending a process that is holding sensitive input. The normal terminal settings and bracketed\-paste mode are restored on exit/errors.
.PP
Long values use a width\-bounded horizontal viewport with a count, so they do not normally wrap; only the current field is displayed, never prior vault values. UTF\-8 input is preserved exactly, with byte limits applied before insertion. Terminal control/bidi/combining characters are never executed/rendered as active terminal sequences when revealed. Bracketed paste treats its contents as data, not toggle/edit/submit keystrokes: multiline, control\-character, invalid UTF\-8, or oversized bracketed pastes are rejected as a whole. Correct/reenter rejected input before submission (Ctrl+U clears it); it is not silently truncated. A terminal without bracketed\-paste support cannot distinguish a pasted newline from Enter, so paste only a single\-line token with no trailing newline.
.PP
The field is erased on completion/cancellation, including when revealed. This does not undo exposure to terminal recordings, screen capture/sharing, or scrollback/reflow. A visible value is an intentional display of a secret on your host terminal, not a way to export or log it; mask/count feedback also discloses length. No prompt input is written to stdout/stderr, shell history, registry, or plaintext temporary files, and the encrypted vault format is unchanged.
.PP
Optional cryptography support is required only when using vault operations:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
pipx install 'oc\-box[vault]'
# Add support to an existing pipx installation:
pipx inject oc\-box 'cryptography>=44'
# Or install/reinstall the uv tool with its extra:
uv tool install \-\-force 'oc\-box[vault]'
# In a package installation's activated virtual environment:
python \-m pip install 'oc\-box[vault]'
.fam T
.fi
.PP
For GitHub/source installations, \fBpython3 install.py \-\-with\-vault\fR stages the optional crypto wheels privately in that release (using a temporary venv), not in system/user\-site Python. The curl bootstrap accepts \fBsh \-s \-\- \-\-with\-vault\fR when piped to a shell. Future GitHub upgrades preserve that opt\-in. The source can also use a host Python with a distro\-provided cryptography package. A missing dependency yields guidance, not a fallback to weak/custom encryption. No desktop keyring, external password manager, cloud service, or daemon is used.
.PP
AES\-256\-GCM authenticates the ciphertext and format/KDF metadata; Scrypt uses a random 16\-byte salt with n=131072, r=8, p=1 (approximately 128 MiB memory) and a 32\-byte key. Each write generates a fresh salt and 12\-byte nonce. Crypto is implemented by the maintained cryptography library, not handwritten primitives. Format version 1 accepts only those bounded parameters; tampered costs cannot trigger arbitrary memory allocation. New/changed passphrases default to a minimum of 8 characters, configurable with \fBvault_min_passphrase_length\fR in the host oc\-box config or \fB\-\-min\-passphrase\-length\fR on \fBinit\fR/\fBpasswd\fR\&. Integers from 8 to 1024 are accepted; longer passwords are allowed up to 1024 UTF\-8 bytes. Existing passphrases unlock regardless of the current creation minimum. Entries are limited to 128, each nonempty UTF\-8 value at most 64 KiB with no NUL, and the complete encrypted file to 2 MiB. Names, unlike workspace selections, are encrypted too.
.PP
The host vault normally resides at \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:oc\-box/\:credentials/\:vault.json\fR, or its XDG_DATA_HOME equivalent. Its directory must be owned by your user with 0700 and the regular vault file with private 0600 permissions. Symlink paths, extra hard links, wrong ownership, insecure permissions, invalid formats, and nonregular vault/lock files are rejected. Files are locked during read\-modify\-write. Ciphertext is written to a 0600 temporary file, fsynced, and atomically replaced; no plaintext temp file or key file is created. Do not locate the vault inside a mounted project.
.PP
There is no passphrase recovery or permanent unlock/cache agent. Forgotten passphrases make the encrypted data unrecoverable. Back up ciphertext and store the passphrase separately. Removing an entry or changing a passphrase does not erase older backups/snapshots or revoke the provider token. WSL requires a host terminal and filesystem permissions such as its Linux home filesystem; this is not a promise that an arbitrary Windows/shared mount enforces Unix permissions. Python memory is not guaranteed zeroized; core dumps are disabled on secret prompt entry where permitted, but host debugging/swap/hardware compromise and secrets delivered to container processes are outside at\-rest encryption. During vault\-enabled launches, host captured subprocess output uses anonymous Linux memfd storage rather than disk\-backed temporary files. Launcher\-generated failure messages redact exact active vault values. This is not a general redaction filter for OpenCode, provider logs, tool output, or session archives: an agent can still print, save, or transmit a credential it has received.
.SS credentials
.PP
View or change **names\-only** forwarding policy for an existing workspace. The default is \fBenv\fR: auto\-forward sourced token/API\-key variables and configured \fBpass_env\fR exactly as before. No policy change occurs merely by upgrading. With no selection options, print mode and selected names without unlocking the vault, revealing values, starting a container, or refreshing config/auth.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box credentials \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box credentials \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-credential\-mode selected \e
  \-\-env\-secret GITHUB_TOKEN \-\-vault\-secret PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Requires the registered project/marker and exclusive workspace lock. Exit its launcher before configuration; changes take effect on the next launch without reset or session import. Selectors default to the current directory; no new workspace is created here. Start a new environment normally once to register it, exit, then configure selections. Use only selectors and credential options; session, device, resource, deletion, or update options are refused.
.PP
In \fBselected\fR mode, only the explicitly selected env/vault names are delivered, plus the launcher's fixed runtime settings and noncredential language/color/ model\-routing preferences. This replaces automatic suffix forwarding and global \fBpass_env\fR, not just supplements them. Vault and env names cannot overlap. Uppercase credential names cannot override loader/shell/host runtime settings such as PATH, BASH_ENV, LD_PRELOAD, OPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT, or OC_BOX variables. Names append/deduplicate; \fB\-\-clear\-credentials\fR clears both lists before adding.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
source /path/to/private/file.env
oc\-box credentials \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-credential\-mode selected \e
  \-\-clear\-credentials \-\-env\-secret GITHUB_TOKEN \-\-vault\-secret BRAVE_API_KEY
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box credentials \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-credential\-mode env \-\-clear\-credentials
.fam T
.fi
.PP
A normal start/reset/import launch unlocks once when selected vault names exist, and the decrypted selection is kept only in the launcher's memory/context. It is not written to the workspace metadata, host os.environ, command arguments, config files, or a cache. Values reach the container through Podman's inherited env names. Wrong passphrase/missing selection aborts before container setup or reset deletion. Env mode may also supplement inherited variables with selected vault names; a vault value then wins over the inherited same\-named env value.
.PP
Informational commands, maintenance terminal, explicit history sync, stop, repair, and upgrade never unlock. The maintenance shell's clean environment rules remain unchanged. Configuration/auth copying is an independent feature: these selections do not remove embedded credentials in copied config, provider or MCP auth files, project files, raw Podman create\-time Env settings, previous processes, or previously saved container data. Selection is reduced forwarding, not an isolation boundary from secrets already inside the container. Delivered keys remain readable by the agent/tools with normal container permissions.
.SS backup
.PP
Create one encrypted \fB\&.ocbox\fR file for an existing workspace (available since 1.0.0). Requires host age/age\-keygen, Python, rootless Podman, and Linux memfd support; it does not require the vault crypto extra or unlock the credential vault. The command must be first, with its own options after it. Use \-\-project/\-\-workspace or the current directory.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box backup \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-output /backup/demo.ocbox
oc\-box backup \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-portable \-\-output /backup/demo\-portable.ocbox
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The workspace lock requires its active OpenCode launcher or maintenance shell to exit. The command verifies marker/physical identity and resource ownership, then stops the container if needed; it remains stopped afterward. Host editors, builds, and other processes must not change project files during capture. The launcher lock cannot quiesce unrelated host writers. Project and container configuration must be intact; missing containers and unknown extra bind mounts/ volumes are refused rather than silently omit outside data.
.PP
Full backup (default) captures the complete project including \fB\&.git\fR, untracked files and unfinished edits; the private home with the live OpenCode SQLite DB, subagent transcripts, snapshots, copied config/auth, and caches; registry/resume evidence; and a committed OCI image of the modified container OS. Mounted project and home are backed up separately, not folded into the image. Portable mode omits that OS image; system packages/SDKs must be recreated after restore. Neither mode captures running processes, kernel state, devices, host drivers, the host\-wide vault, host global DB/config, or arbitrary paths outside those components. Env files inside the selected project are project data and are included; host env files outside it are not read.
.PP
Default encryption asks for a new/confirmed masked backup passphrase (default minimum 8 characters), separate from the vault password. Set \fBbackup_min_passphrase_length\fR in host oc\-box config or use \fB\-\-min\-passphrase\-length N\fR for this backup. Both accept integers from 8 to 1024; longer passwords are allowed up to 1024 UTF\-8 bytes. Blank/short/mismatched input is rejected, rather than asking age to generate a password. oc\-box passes the validated password to standard age through a private, echo\-disabled terminal; no password enters argv, environment variables, or plaintext files. Age still provides the cipher/KDF and prompts directly when unlocking existing backups, which are not restricted by the current creation minimum. For unattended encryption use a native age X25519 public recipient, repeatable:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
age\-keygen \-o ~/.config/backup\-age\-key.txt
age\-keygen \-y ~/.config/backup\-age\-key.txt
oc\-box backup \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-recipient age1PUBLIC_KEY \e
  \-\-output /backup/demo.ocbox
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The generated private key file is sensitive and should remain 0600 outside project/container access. The public recipient may be stored in a scheduler. Keep the private identity/backup passphrase separately; losing it makes the backup unrecoverable. Age plugins/SSH identities are not accepted in format v1.
.PP
The envelope contains fixed\-name age\-encrypted components plus an encrypted manifest and age\-protected per\-backup private key. The manifest authenticates component SHA\-256 digests/sizes; all components use age authenticated streaming encryption. Plaintext streams through pipes, and secret keys/captured stderr use anonymous memory. Temporary backup staging contains ciphertext only. At most 16 TiB ciphertext/expanded archive bytes per component and 2 million entries are accepted in format v1. The output parent must exist outside the project, registry, and private home. New output is published atomically without replacing an existing file and has 0600 permissions. Expect enough free space for staged ciphertext, final output, and a temporary committed image. Failure leaves no final partial backup; the source remains stopped, not reset/deleted. The temporary capture image is removed by its unique tag; shared base images remain.
.SS backup\-info
.PP
Decrypt/verify a backup's manifest and ciphertext checksums without starting Podman or extracting workspace contents. Requires age and a controlling terminal for passphrase backups, or a native private \-\-identity file for recipient backups. Output is JSON metadata including kind, source path/ID, architecture, component sizes/checksums, saved session IDs, settings, and documented limitations. Raw additional Podman option values are omitted to avoid printing saved secrets. It never prints archived file contents or the host vault and does not execute container image code. Metadata itself may contain private paths/session names.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box backup\-info /backup/demo.ocbox
oc\-box backup\-info /backup/demo.ocbox \-\-identity ~/.config/backup\-age\-key.txt
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Inspection stages only encrypted envelope components while checking them. It authenticates the manifest and binds component ciphertext to it; restore also verifies every age data stream through its final authentication chunk. Wrong keys, corruption, missing/extra components, unsupported versions, or unsafe outer envelope paths are rejected before restore activation.
.SS restore
.PP
Restore into a **new, nonexistent** host project destination. Never overwrites an existing directory or workspace; there is no \-\-force/\-\-replace mode. Run on the host as your regular user after installing age. The active source workspace does not need to be removed: this is a fresh independent environment.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box restore /backup/demo.ocbox \-\-project ~/devel/demo\-restored
oc\-box restore /backup/demo.ocbox \-\-project ~/devel/demo\-restored \e
  \-\-identity ~/.config/backup\-age\-key.txt
.fam T
.fi
.PP
It decrypts/verifies the manifest, checks architecture/settings, stages project files in a private sibling directory, creates a fresh labeled home volume, and streams its extraction under podman unshare for container UID/GID mappings. Registry content is retained as \fBrecovered\-registry/\:\fR evidence, not activated as arbitrary create commands or old host\-archive paths. Then a full backup loads the captured OCI image; portable mode selects the original distro catalog image. Only after component restoration succeeds does atomic no\-replace rename publish the project and record its actual destination identity with fresh IDs.
.PP
The new environment is **created but not started**. Its resume selection keeps the original conversation IDs and complete container DB. Original private home and in\-container project paths remain the same; \fBcontainer_project\fR in metadata can differ from the new host project. Existing session snapshots/tool paths need no database rewrite. Status reports both paths when different. Host archive sync from a restored workspace uses fresh namespaced archive IDs and the new host project. Old host archive associations are not reused/deleted.
.PP
Normal startup still copies the current host config/auth and checks the exact host OpenCode version. The archive's device/extra Podman settings are recorded as requirements but **not automatically activated**. The restored image's environment and declared volumes are cleared, image healthchecks are disabled, and a fixed entrypoint is used; backup image hooks, startup commands, external mounts, and host device settings are not trusted as new host launch privileges. Full restores retain OS files, which are executable code when you later start/use that environment: restore only trusted backups. Portable restores recreate baseline tools on first normal launch and need custom system dependencies reinstalled. Host drivers and CPU architecture are not portable; this initial format requires a matching CPU architecture even for portable mode because cached home binaries may be native.
.PP
Extraction refuses traversal, duplicate/excessive entries, special devices/ FIFOs/sockets, sparse extensions, oversized metadata, hardlinks outside earlier regular entries, and any write through a symlink ancestor. Project files become owned by the current host user; private\-home ownership is mapped in the rootless namespace. Regular modes/timestamps/symlinks/hardlinks are kept, but setuid/setgid/ sticky bits, ACLs, xattrs, and source SELinux labels are not restored. New project mounts use Podman's normal :z relabeling. Symlink targets may name outside paths but are never followed during extraction; check them before later host use. Restored project and staging roots are kept private (0700), even if the original root had broader permissions; permissions of ordinary files/subdirectories remain.
.PP
The host vault is not included or unlocked. Credential selections retain names only, so provide matching host env/vault credentials before normal selected\-mode startup. Copied auth/config and secrets stored in project/home/OS are part of the encrypted payload and remain present after restore. A failed operation cleans only its newly created project/volume/container/registry and preserves original environments; interruption may retain staging marked incomplete, which normal launch refuses until the operator inspects/retries. Loaded OCI images may remain in shared Podman image cache after failure; no global prune is used. The host registry journals fresh resource IDs and \fBrestore_staging_project\fR before payload extraction. An interrupted or failed\-cleanup entry has \fBrestore_incomplete: true\fR; list/status can expose its ID without launching it. Inspect the staging location, use plain \fBpurge \-\-workspace ID\fR for its managed resources (which skips history sync for incomplete restores), and retry to a new destination. Orphan staging directories may need manual host removal after inspection; the launcher never deletes a caller\-supplied tree on marker alone.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS \-h, \-\-help
.PP
Print command choices, options, and short descriptions; exit without Podman. For example: \fBoc\-box \-\-help\fR\&.
.SS \-\-min\-passphrase\-length N
.PP
Set the minimum character count for a new vault or backup passphrase for this operation. Accepted only with \fBoc\-box secrets init\fR, \fBoc\-box secrets passwd\fR, and \fBoc\-box backup\fR; it is incompatible with backup \fB\-\-recipient\fR\&. N must be an integer from 8 to 1024. The default is 8 unless the corresponding host config key \fBvault_min_passphrase_length\fR or \fBbackup_min_passphrase_length\fR is set; this flag overrides that key without saving it. Passwords may exceed the minimum, subject to the 1024 UTF\-8\-byte limit. Unlock/restore never enforces a new creation policy against an existing password. The number is public policy, not a way to pass a password in argv. For example:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box secrets init \-\-min\-passphrase\-length 8
oc\-box secrets passwd \-\-min\-passphrase\-length 16
oc\-box backup \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-output /backup/demo.ocbox \e
  \-\-min\-passphrase\-length 12
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-feature FEATURE
.PP
Doctor\-only, repeatable. Limit the dependency report and exit\-status evaluation to named features. \fBoc\-box doctor \-\-feature backup\-info\fR checks age and memory support without requiring Podman or host OpenCode. With no selector, report all features and evaluate launch prerequisites for the exit status. Use \fBoc\-box doctor \-\-help\fR for the complete accepted names.
.SS \-\-project PATH
.PP
Select an existing project directory; relative paths resolve from the host working directory. Quote paths containing spaces. For lifecycle commands the default is the current directory. For status, omission means all workspaces. The project must not be the host home, \fB/\:\fR, \fB/\:home\fR, the oc\-box registry, or a directory containing trusted launcher/state files. Colons, commas, and line breaks in the project path are unsupported because of Podman mount syntax.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project "$HOME/devel/my project"
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-workspace ID
.PP
Select an existing workspace by its full 32\-character ID from \fBoc\-box list\fR\&. This works from another directory and allows cleanup after the project is deleted. Starting, syncing, or resetting still needs its original project directory. The identity marker and registry are not interchangeable with OpenCode session IDs. Example: \fBoc\-box \-\-status \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID\fR\&.
.SS \-\-status
.PP
Report CPU, RAM, storage, state, limits, and sessions without opening OpenCode. Equivalent to \fBstatus\fR\&. Only selectors and \fB\-\-json\fR apply. It cannot be combined with another command, session mode, resource change, or deletion action.
.SS \-\-json
.PP
Doctor also accepts this flag to emit its versioned feature/dependency report.
.PP
Emit status as one JSON object with \fBsampled_at\fR and \fBworkspaces\fR\&. Sizes are numeric bytes and unavailable values are \fBnull\fR; notes explain partial results. Progress goes to stderr, not the JSON stream. Status uses this schema; doctor uses the feature/dependency schema described under that command.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-json > status.json
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-about
.PP
Print oc\-box version, author Paulo Dias and email, OpenAI coding\-assistant contribution, GPL\-3.0\-or\-later licensing, and source commit. No Podman or workspace is required. Use alone, without lifecycle selectors/options.
.SS \-\-version
.PP
Print oc\-box's commit\-derived version and exit without Podman. The format is \fB1.0.N+gHASH\fR, with N the full\-history commit count minus 29 (the 1.0.0 baseline). Each later commit advances N; shallow history cannot supply a release identity. Tracked uncommitted changes add \fB\&.dirty\fR\&. Untracked files do not. Installed copies retain their installation\-time identity in \fBbuild\-info.json\fR; reinstall to update it. This is separate from the host OpenCode version matched inside containers. Use alone, for example: \fBoc\-box \-\-version\fR\&.
.PP
PyPI distribution metadata uses only \fB1.0.N\fR, because public PyPI versions cannot contain the local \fB+gHASH\fR suffix. Its packaged build\-info still includes the full source identity displayed by this command, so the exact Git commit is retained even in a wheel/source distribution installed without Git. Existing 0.1 installations retain their original embedded identity until upgraded through their usual channel. The absolute commit count remains in metadata for upgrade ordering; no workspace reset or database migration is required for the version\-series transition.
.SS \-\-distro NAME
.PP
Choose a supported distro for a new environment. The default matches the host ID when supported; openSUSE Tumbleweed/Slowroll use Tumbleweed, other openSUSE variants use Leap, and unknown hosts fall back to Tumbleweed. The image is a distribution userland, not an exact clone of host packages or snapshot date. Changing an existing workspace requires \fBreset \-\-distro NAME\fR\&.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro tumbleweed
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro debian
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-check
.PP
With \fBupgrade\fR, report whether the public GitHub commit differs from the installed version without installing it. Returns success when a version is available or the installation is current; it is not an update\-available exit code. Network errors return nonzero. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-auto\-update\fR, workspace selectors, or other commands. Example: \fBoc\-box upgrade \-\-check\fR\&.
.SS \-\-auto\-update on|off
.PP
With \fBupgrade\fR, persistently enable or disable automatic updates before normal installed launches. The default is off. This setting itself performs no download or installation and preserves other keys in \fB~/\:.config/\:oc\-box/\:config.json\fR (or its XDG equivalent). Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-check\fR or workspace actions. Example: \fBoc\-box upgrade \-\-auto\-update on\fR\&.
.SS \-\-new
.PP
Start a new root conversation while retaining the same container and files. Use with start (the default) or reset. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-resume\fR and \fB\-\-import\-session\fR\&. Example: \fBoc\-box \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-new\fR\&.
.SS \-\-resume
.PP
Explicitly request the normal resume behavior. Requires a registered workspace; if one exists but no root session has been recorded, OpenCode can start a new conversation. Use with start/reset. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-new\fR and \fB\-\-import\-session\fR\&. Example: \fBoc\-box \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-resume\fR\&.
.SS \-\-import\-session SESSION_ID
.PP
Copy a host conversation from the selected project into the private container database, verify the transcript, select the exact ID, and launch it. Start\-only; mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-new\fR and \fB\-\-resume\fR\&. Quit the source host client first to include its final saved turn. A subagent session cannot be imported as a root. The original host conversation is retained. See Sessions and recovery. An oc\-box host archive can also be imported into a fresh environment for that same project; the original registry and saved archive verify its project.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-model PROVIDER/MODEL
.PP
Pass the provider/model choice to the OpenCode TUI on this launch. It is not a persistent oc\-box workspace setting. Use with start/reset; otherwise normal OpenCode provider/model preferences apply.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-model github\-copilot/gpt\-6.1\-sol
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-credential\-mode env|selected
.PP
With \fBcredentials\fR, choose forwarding mode for that workspace. \fBenv\fR is the unchanged default: suffix\-based and custom pass_env forwarding, optionally supplemented with selected vault names. \fBselected\fR forwards only configured credential names, plus fixed runtime/nonsecret routing settings. The mode is saved in host metadata, not applied as a container reset or to an active client. Example: \fBoc\-box credentials \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-credential\-mode selected\fR\&.
.SS \-\-env\-secret NAME
.PP
With \fBcredentials\fR, append an uppercase environment credential name to the workspace's explicit env selection, repeatable and deduplicated. In selected mode it must be present/nonempty in the sourced host environment before launch; a missing value aborts before unlock/setup. Env mode already forwards legacy variables, so this list becomes restrictive only in selected mode. No value is accepted here. Example: \fBoc\-box credentials \-\-env\-secret PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN\fR\&.
.SS \-\-vault\-secret NAME
.PP
With \fBcredentials\fR, select an existing encrypted vault entry name to unlock and forward on normal start/reset/import launches. Repeat for multiple names. The name is saved in workspace metadata, never its secret value. Vault presence is checked when unlocking, not by this names\-only configuration command. A name cannot also be selected with \-\-env\-secret. Example: \fBoc\-box credentials \-\-vault\-secret BRAVE_API_KEY\fR\&.
.SS \-\-clear\-credentials
.PP
With \fBcredentials\fR, clear both saved env\-name and vault\-name lists before adding new selections on this invocation. It does not erase the vault, remove provider auth/config, revoke credentials, or change mode unless \-\-credential\-mode is also specified. Example: \fBoc\-box credentials \-\-credential\-mode env \-\-clear\-credentials\fR returns to the original env workflow without deleting encrypted vault entries.
.SS \-\-output FILE
.PP
Backup\-only. Required new encrypted backup output pathname. Its parent must already exist outside the project/registry/private volume. Existing files or symlinks are never overwritten, including a destination created during capture. Example: \fBoc\-box backup \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-output /\:backup/\:demo.ocbox\fR\&. The archive suffix is conventional; the versioned format is checked on restore.
.SS \-\-portable
.PP
Backup\-only. Omit the container OS image while retaining project, complete private home, sessions, registry evidence, and limits/credential\-name settings. Restore rebuilds the selected distro OS/baseline tools; custom system packages and native\-cache compatibility are the user's responsibility. Example: \fBoc\-box backup \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-portable \-\-output /\:backup/\:demo.ocbox\fR\&.
.SS \-\-recipient AGE_KEY
.PP
Backup\-only, repeatable (up to 16). Encrypt the backup key envelope to a native age X25519 public key, allowing backup without a password prompt. Any matching private identity can decrypt it. Without this option age requests a backup passphrase interactively. Public recipients are not secret; private identities must be stored independently of the archive and project. Example: \fBoc\-box backup \-\-recipient age1PUBLIC_KEY \-\-output /\:backup/\:demo.ocbox\fR\&.
.SS \-\-identity FILE
.PP
Restore/backup\-info only, repeatable. Read a native age X25519 private identity file owned by the host user with private permissions, no symlink or extra hard links. Plugins, SSH identities, and encrypted identity files are refused in the initial format; passphrase\-encrypted backups instead use age's terminal prompt. No identity text or passphrase is accepted through argv/environment. Example: \fBoc\-box backup\-info /\:backup/\:demo.ocbox \-\-identity ~/\:.config/\:backup\-age\-key.txt\fR\&.
.SS \-\-cpus NUMBER
.PP
Set the CPU quota in core units; fractions such as \fB2.5\fR are accepted by Podman. Default: \fB4\fR\&. Set at creation or reset. Existing containers, even stopped ones, require reset to change it; oc\-box does not call \fBpodman update\fR\&. Values are validated by Podman. \fB0\fR requests no CPU quota where supported.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-cpus 8
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-memory SIZE
.PP
Set the memory limit using Podman size syntax, such as \fB512m\fR, \fB8g\fR, or \fB16g\fR\&. Default: \fB8g\fR\&. Set at creation or reset; not a live update. The value is saved for future launches. Limits require delegated cgroup controllers. Podman validates the size and kernel support. \fB0\fR requests no memory limit where supported. There is no automatic project/volume disk quota.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-memory 16g
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-pids\-limit NUMBER
.PP
Set the container's process limit; default \fB2048\fR\&. oc\-box requires at least \fB32\fR\&. Set at creation or reset; changing an existing container requires reset. Example: \fBoc\-box reset \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-pids\-limit 4096\fR\&.
.SS \-\-device MAPPING
.PP
Add a persistent Podman mapping, repeatable. Supports \fB/\:host/\:device[:/\:container/\:device][:rwm]\fR and CDI \fBvendor/\:class=name\fR\&. New settings append to saved mappings; identical strings are deduplicated. Changing existing mappings requires reset. Use \fB\-\-clear\-devices\fR to replace or remove them. Host permissions, SELinux, and installed driver userland still apply; device mapping alone does not install an NPU/GPU SDK.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/gpu \-\-device /dev/dri/renderD128:rw
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-OPTION[=VALUE]
.PP
Append one literal long\-option token to the Podman create command before the image. Repeat the flag for multiple options. Use \fB=\fR both after \fB\-\-podman\-arg\fR and within valued Podman options; this is not a shell command string or a space\-split list. Values containing spaces remain one argument when quoted. Saved options persist across launches/resets; identical strings are deduplicated. Remove old/conflicting options with \fB\-\-clear\-podman\-args\fR when replacing them.
.PP
The following are reserved by oc\-box: \fB\-\-name\fR, \fB\-\-label\fR, \fB\-\-label\-file\fR, \fB\-\-cidfile\fR, \fB\-\-replace\fR, \fB\-\-rm\fR, \fB\-\-entrypoint\fR, \fB\-\-workdir\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR, \fB\-\-rootfs\fR, \fB\-\-pod\fR, and \fB\-\-pod\-id\-file\fR\&. Use oc\-box's dedicated CPU, memory, and process flags instead of forwarding their Podman equivalents. Options are for \fBpodman create\fR, not global Podman flags or arguments after the image.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-shm\-size=1g \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-ulimit=memlock=\-1:\-1
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Extra devices, mounts, capabilities, host networking, and security options extend the default boundary as requested by the host operator. Podman and the host's rootless permissions determine which settings are available.
.SS \-\-clear\-devices
.PP
Clear all saved \fB\-\-device\fR mappings before adding any new ones on this command. Use during reset for an existing container. It does not remove a device passed as a raw Podman option; use \fB\-\-clear\-podman\-args\fR for that list.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-clear\-devices
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-clear\-devices \-\-device /dev/accel/accel1
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-clear\-podman\-args
.PP
Clear saved additional Podman options before appending replacements. Use during reset for an existing container. It leaves the dedicated device list unchanged.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-clear\-podman\-args \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-shm\-size=2g
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-delete\-sessions
.PP
Purge\-only. Delete recorded host archive session copies as well as environment resources. Project files remain unless \fB\-\-all\fR is used. It never deletes an original host source conversation merely because that conversation was imported into this workspace. Example: \fBoc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-delete\-sessions\fR\&.
.SS \-\-all
.PP
Purge\-only. Remove environment resources, recorded host session archives, metadata, and the entire original project directory, including untracked files. The directory identity and marker must still match. Example: \fBoc\-box purge \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:disposable \-\-all\fR\&.
.SS \-y, \-\-yes
.PP
Skip purge's ID\-prefix confirmation. Purge\-only; it does not answer OpenCode questions or bypass permissions on other commands. Example: \fBoc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-all \-\-yes\fR\&.
.SH LIFECYCLE AND PERSISTENCE
.PP
.nf
.fam C
Operation              Project   Private home/sessions   Container OS
Exit OpenCode          kept      kept; archive sync     kept; stopped
stop                   kept      kept                   kept; stopped
sync                   kept      kept; archive sync     kept; stopped
terminal               kept      kept; no archive sync  kept; prior run state
reset                  kept      kept, same session ID  rebuilt
purge                  kept      deleted                deleted
purge \-\-delete\-sessions kept      deleted                deleted
purge \-\-all            deleted   deleted                deleted
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Default purge retains host archive copies; \fB\-\-delete\-sessions\fR and \fB\-\-all\fR remove recorded host archive copies. Imported source host conversations remain. Shared base images are retained by all operations. Stop/reset do not checkpoint running processes. Packages installed in the private home survive reset; system\-wide packages do not. No command currently manages Btrfs snapshots.
.PP
CPU, RAM, process, distro, and mapping changes require reset for existing containers. A stop/start alone reuses the old create\-time settings. Avoid changing resources directly through Podman if metadata consistency is required. An active launcher holds a nonblocking workspace lock; start, sync, reset, repair, terminal, and purge require that launcher to exit. Read\-only list/status and explicit stop can run from a second terminal. A crash releases the lock when its process exits; an orphan still running can retain it.
.SH DISTRIBUTION IMAGES
.SS Host dependency installation
.PP
Host requirements and workspace baseline packages are separate. These commands cover core rootless tools and optional age backup tools; doctor prints only packages missing for each feature. Run OS package commands manually with the privileges appropriate to your machine (\fBsudo\fR on a configured regular host, or as root without sudo in an administrative shell).
.PP
| Host distro | Core rootless prerequisites plus optional backup tools | | \-\-\- | \-\-\- | | Tumbleweed | \fBsudo zypper refresh && sudo zypper install python3 podman passt shadow catatonit conmon crun age\fR | | Leap | \fBsudo zypper refresh && sudo zypper install python3 podman passt shadow catatonit conmon runc age\fR | | Ubuntu / Debian | \fBsudo apt update && sudo apt install python3 podman passt uidmap catatonit conmon crun age\fR | | Fedora | \fBsudo dnf install python3 podman passt shadow\-utils catatonit conmon crun age\fR | | Arch | \fBsudo pacman \-Syu \-\-needed python podman passt shadow catatonit conmon crun age\fR | | Alpine | \fBsudo apk add python3 podman passt shadow\-subids catatonit conmon crun age\fR |
.PP
Installing packages alone does not configure subordinate IDs, cgroups, or make rootful Podman rootless. Alpine requires its release's community repository for some providers; Ubuntu may require its official universe repository. Use a current supported OS with Python 3.10+ and pasta\-capable Podman; an older Leap/ Python combination may need an OS upgrade rather than a package reinstall.
.PP
| Feature | Host dependencies | | \-\-\- | \-\-\- | | Launch/reset/sync | Linux, running Python 3.10+, Podman, pasta (\fBpasst\fR), newuidmap/newgidmap, catatonit, conmon, crun or runc, host OpenCode 1.x | | Maintenance/stop/repair/purge | Linux, Python, Podman and UID helpers; history preservation/deletion additionally uses host OpenCode | | Full/portable backup | Linux, Python, rootless Podman/UID helpers, age/age\-keygen (both from \fBage\fR), memfd support | | Backup inspection | Linux, Python, age/age\-keygen, memfd; no Podman/OpenCode/vault extra | | Restore | Backup prerequisites plus pasta, init/helper/runtime providers for container creation | | Optional vault | \fBcryptography\fR in the same Python environment as oc\-box; no age requirement | | GitHub installation/upgrade | Git and Python; one\-line download also needs curl, mktemp and trusted CA certificates | | Storage reports | GNU \fBdu\fR from \fBcoreutils\fR; BusyBox du is insufficient; other metrics still work if absent | | FUSE overlay driver | \fBfuse\-overlayfs\fR only if selected/needed; kernel overlay does not require it | | Read manuals | man (\fBman\fR, \fBman\-db\fR, or Alpine \fBmandoc\fR) and info (\fBinfo\fR or \fBtexinfo\fR) | | Regenerate manuals | GNU makeinfo (\fBtexinfo\fR); contributor\-only, never required to install/use oc\-box |
.PP
Host OpenCode is not supplied by these mappings; see https://opencode.ai/docs/#install. Use the existing Python installation channel for vault support: \fBuv tool install \-\-force 'oc\-box[vault]'\fR, \fBpipx inject oc\-box 'cryptography>=44'\fR, or GitHub/source \fB\-\-with\-vault\fR\&. Do not install Python dependencies into system Python to fix an isolated tool's missing extra.
.PP
Inside each workspace, oc\-box automatically installs Bash, CA certificates, curl, Git, Python, ripgrep, tar/gzip, Node/npm, and PDF tools (\fBpoppler\-tools\fR on openSUSE, \fBpoppler\-utils\fR on Debian/Ubuntu/Fedora/Alpine, \fBpoppler\fR on Arch). uv/uvx and matching OpenCode are bootstrapped inside private storage. MCPs, formatters, language servers, plugins and project libraries may have their own requirements; doctor does not infer those from project configuration.
.PP
Distro CI installs mapped packages in disposable instances of all seven catalog images, checks actual command providers, tests standard age, and verifies scoped guidance. It does not run nested rootless Podman or replace host lifecycle tests.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
Name         Image
tumbleweed   registry.opensuse.org/opensuse/tumbleweed:latest
leap         registry.opensuse.org/opensuse/leap:latest
ubuntu       docker.io/library/ubuntu:24.04
debian       docker.io/library/debian:stable
fedora       registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora:latest
arch         docker.io/library/archlinux:base
alpine       docker.io/library/alpine:latest
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Aliases: \fBopensuse\fR and \fBopensuse\-tumbleweed\fR select \fBtumbleweed\fR; \fBopensuse\-leap\fR selects \fBleap\fR; \fBarchlinux\fR selects \fBarch\fR\&. \fBoc\-box distros\fR reports the actual compiled catalog. Images are pulled when missing, not automatically refreshed to a newer remote tag on every launch.
.SH SESSIONS AND RECOVERY
.PP
The live database is private container storage. The normal host database is not mounted. Normal exit and \fBsync\fR export root conversations using supported mainline commands, then import namespaced host archive IDs with titles prefixed \fB[oc\-box project\-name]\fR\&. Repeated sync refreshes the archive copy. Resume uses the original container session, not that archive ID.
.PP
This is conversation\-history sync, not live replication or a backup against disk loss. Host archives omit snapshot stores, separate subagent transcripts, running processes, installed packages, and files outside the exported transcript. New work in a host archive is not merged back automatically. Container/home storage normally survives a client crash or reboot; the last uncommitted turn can still be lost. Retry missed archival with \fBoc\-box sync\fR\&.
.PP
For \fB\-\-import\-session\fR, quit the source client first. Its project must match the selected directory. A private source export is saved in \fBimports/\:SESSION_ID.json\fR under the workspace registry. oc\-box imports into the container, verifies every message/part, then launches that exact ID. Mainline's removal of obsolete nonpending tool \fBstate.raw\fR buffers is accepted, but tool inputs/outputs, pending raw buffers, text, IDs, and metadata remain checked.
.PP
Do not routinely repeat the import flag. An already verified ID is not merged or overwritten. If verification was interrupted/rejected, retry the same import after updating the launcher: an existing session may be recovered by comparison with the original saved export, without reimporting over container work. Differences outside the known normalization stop launch and retain both copies.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box sync \-\-project ~/devel/demo
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The source\-tree diagnostic prints counts and differing field names, not text:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
python3 tools/diagnose_import.py \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-session SESSION_ID
.fam T
.fi
.SS Recover history after replacing a workspace marker
.PP
Older launchers allowed removing \fB\&.oc\-box\-id\fR and starting again to create a fresh environment. Current launchers detect the registered path and refuse to create a duplicate; run \fBrepair\fR to reconnect the existing environment instead. Removing the marker does not delete the old private volume, registry, or host archives. If an older launcher already created a replacement, to continue a previously synced conversation in the new environment, exit its active client, find the host archive title prefixed \fB[oc\-box project\-name]\fR, and import its ID:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
opencode \-\-pure session list
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_ARCHIVE_ID
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Mainline records host archive imports against oc\-box's private \fBcontroller\fR directory. oc\-box verifies the archive's namespaced ID against that workspace, its original host registry, and its saved \fBhistory/\:SESSION_ID.json\fR before restoring the project directory for transfer. Both saved records must identify the selected project. The current host transcript is copied, retaining any later host archive work; the host session and saved archive are not changed. The archive title alone cannot authorize a different project.
.PP
After a plain purge with a current launcher, the same checks use its private \fBretired/\:ID/\:\fR recovery records if the original active registry is gone. Older purges that removed those records cannot be reconstructed automatically: use a retained backup or a newer archive from the live workspace. Existing container sessions remain usable without the retired registry; that registry is needed only for verifying a host archive's old controller\-directory origin.
.PP
Use the import flag once, then resume normally. If the original registry or saved export is missing, restore it before retrying. Host history contains only successfully synced turns; the old private volume may contain newer work. Keep the original workspace resources until recovery is complete. Importing history does not copy installed packages, snapshots, or other private\-home files.
.SS Retire a stale duplicate without deleting the current project
.PP
Inspect the marker association and recorded sessions before cleanup:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
oc\-box \-\-status
oc\-box purge \-\-workspace STALE_WORKSPACE_ID
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Keep the workspace selected by the current project marker. Plain purge final\-syncs the stale workspace's history and removes only its owned container/volume/network and active registry. It leaves the replacement workspace, project files, current marker, and host conversation archives intact. If the final sync or recovery\-copy write fails, cleanup stops before removing resources. Do not use \fB\-\-all\fR or \fB\-\-delete\-sessions\fR for this history\-preserving duplicate cleanup. oc\-box never chooses and deletes duplicates automatically. If the old workspace has newer unsynced work, recover it before retiring its private home.
.SH DEVICES AND HOST PERMISSIONS
.PP
The host user must be able to access the device. Host supplementary groups are not automatically preserved; where group access is required, use installed crun and \fB\-\-group\-add=keep\-groups\fR\&. A same\-named group inside the container is not the host group. Newly acquired groups require a fresh host login/process with those groups. Driver\-specific SDKs must be installed inside the container.
.PP
Before creating/recreating device\-enabled containers, oc\-box checks requested host read/write access and SELinux's device boolean, including \fB/\:usr/\:sbin\fR utilities that may be absent from PATH. Warnings are advisory; oc\-box does not run sudo or change host policy. The policy warning is skipped when device access is already allowed, SELinux is not enforcing, or the container explicitly bypasses labeling. CDI devices receive the SELinux check, not a host\-path check.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/npu \e
  \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0 \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-runtime=crun \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-group\-add=keep\-groups
.fam T
.fi
.PP
SELinux can deny access even when a mapped device name is visible with \fB\-?????????\fR attributes. From the host, inspect policy and permissions:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
ls \-ldZ /dev/accel
ls \-lZ /dev/accel/accel0
id
/usr/sbin/getenforce
/usr/sbin/getsebool container_use_devices
test \-r /dev/accel/accel0 && test \-w /dev/accel/accel0
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If the user has access and enforcing SELinux has the device boolean off, Podman's documented host\-wide policy setting is:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
sudo /usr/sbin/setsebool \-P container_use_devices on
.fam T
.fi
.PP
It permits device labels subject to mappings and Unix permissions; it does not map extra devices or disable SELinux. It persists and applies immediately, including to running containers, without reset/reboot. A memlock problem does not explain failure to read device attributes. Once access works, inspect the driver and limits independently inside the container:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
ls \-l /dev/accel/accel0
prlimit \-\-pid $$ \-\-memlock
xrt\-smi examine
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Requested memlock limits are still subject to the host user's hard limit and kernel/runtime rules; passing a ulimit does not grant host privileges.
.SH RESOURCE REPORTS
.PP
Status collects running state, CPU, RAM/limit, PIDs, uptime, configured limits, image, storage driver, restart/exit/OOM/health information when available, and the latest saved session and archive IDs. Device mappings and saved raw Podman options are included. No container commands run during collection.
.PP
It also checks the project's regular marker and recorded filesystem identity, identifies multiple registered workspaces for the same project in an all\-workspace report, and includes specific repair or history\-preserving cleanup instructions. JSON fields \fBproject_marker_workspace\fR and \fBrelated_workspace_ids\fR expose those associations. Suspicious/missing projects are not scanned for storage usage. \fBHost archive: not recorded\fR means this workspace has not recorded a successful sync of its selected session yet; the live volume/session remains intact. Exit normally or, after the launcher exits, run \fBsync\fR to create the host archive.
.PP
Storage is split into writable OS layer, private home volume, and project. The rootfs size including shared image layers is separate. Filesystem used/free space and block\-I/O counters are shown. CPU is a short sample where 100% is one core; stopped containers have no live sample. Missing/unavailable values are not reported as zero. Rootless pasta network counters are omitted as unreliable.
.PP
Directory scans count allocated blocks without following symlinks or crossing filesystems, with a 15\-second timeout per directory. Private\-volume scans use \fBpodman unshare\fR on the host, not container exec. Very large caches may take time or report unavailable size. Shared image layers must not be summed as unique storage. Btrfs compression, reflinks, snapshots, and shared extents make these estimates different from exclusive physical usage. There is no disk quota.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-status
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-json > workspace\-status.json
.fam T
.fi
.SH CONFIGURATION AND ENVIRONMENT
.PP
On launch, global OpenCode configuration, agents, skills, local plugins, and MCP settings are copied into private storage; they are not host mounts. Project configuration runs natively inside the container. Removed host plugins are removed from the copy. Container dependency caches and generated SDK locks are reused when host dependency inputs and runtime version are unchanged. Cold plugin resolution has its own timed setup stage before the TUI opens.
.PP
The generated profile selects Bash and enables native formatters/LSP when not explicitly configured. A container\-only plugin sets Build permissions to allow all. Plan/subagent and plain host permissions are unchanged. Other copied plugins may have independent checks. Changes require exiting and restarting OpenCode; config is not hot\-reloaded. Host\-only executables must be installed inside the container. Host provider/MCP auth is copied when available; refreshed container auth is not written back to host auth.
.PP
HTTP endpoint keys in global config using localhost, 127.0.0.1, or ::1 are rewritten to \fBhost.containers.internal\fR\&. OAuth redirect URIs and shell commands are not rewritten. Container localhost remains local to the container.
.SS Environment variables
.PP
\fBOC_BOX_STATE_HOME\fR overrides the host workspace registry root. Otherwise it is \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/\:oc\-box\fR, defaulting to \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box\fR\&. Use the same registry when looking up existing workspace IDs; changing it does not migrate old resources automatically.
.PP
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR selects the source global OpenCode/oc\-box configuration; default \fB~/\:.config\fR\&. \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR selects the normal host OpenCode data root; default \fB~/\:.local/\:share\fR\&. The installer itself uses fixed \fB~/\:.local\fR paths. Container XDG paths use the private home, not host XDG directories.
.PP
Variables ending \fB_API_KEY\fR or \fB_TOKEN\fR, plus \fBGH_TOKEN\fR, \fBGITHUB_TOKEN\fR, terminal color/language settings, and \fBLEMONADE_BASE_URL\fR are passed when set. These forwarding rules apply to normal OpenCode launches, not \fBterminal\fR\&. Add other uppercase environment names in host configuration:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
{
  "pass_env": ["MY_MCP_SETTING", "MY_PROVIDER_SECRET"]
}
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Save that as \fB~/\:.config/\:oc\-box/\:config.json\fR (or its XDG equivalent). Only values present in the host environment are copied. Forwarded HTTP localhost URLs are mapped to the host\-service name. The default \fBLEMONADE_BASE_URL\fR is \fBhttp:/\:/\:host.containers.internal:13305/\:api/\:v1\fR\&. \fBTERM\fR defaults to \fBxterm\-256color\fR\&. Host PATH, shell startup scripts, proxy variables, engine sockets, and SSH agents are not forwarded by default.
.PP
Separate password creation minimums can be saved in the same JSON object:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
{
  "vault_min_passphrase_length": 12,
  "backup_min_passphrase_length": 16
}
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Each defaults to 8 and accepts an integer from 8 to 1024. They set a minimum character count, not an exact size; all passphrases remain bounded to 1024 UTF\-8 bytes. \fB\-\-min\-passphrase\-length N\fR overrides the relevant setting for one creation/change. Invalid policy fails before vault unlock or backup lifecycle changes. Existing passwords and recipient\-encrypted backups do not consult these creation settings. Preserve your other keys when editing this file.
.PP
The same JSON object can include \fB"auto_update": true\fR to opt in to anonymous GitHub checks/installation before normal installed launches, or use \fBoc\-box upgrade \-\-auto\-update on\fR\&. The setting is off by default and is not forwarded into the container. \fBOC_BOX_UPDATE_CHECKED=1\fR is an internal one\-launch guard used when restarting the host launcher after automatic installation; it prevents recursion, not authentication or a workspace identity override.
.PP
Forwarded credential/custom environment values are passed through the Podman client's environment, not embedded in its process arguments. They are still accessible to container processes and same\-user environment inspection. Copied configuration and auth can also contain secrets; only supply credentials the agent should use. See SECURITY.md for the documented trust boundary.
.PP
\fBOPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT\fR can supply provider auth to copy into the container. The launcher sets \fBOC_BOX_CONTAINER=1\fR, \fBOPENCODE_DISABLE_SANDBOX=1\fR, and \fBOPENCODE_DISABLE_AUTOUPDATE=1\fR inside it, plus its private profile/config. OpenCode's existing Bubblewrap plugin is disabled only for this launch; Podman is the environment boundary. No OpenCode fork is installed.
.SH FILES
.SS Host installation
.PP
\fB~/\:.local/\:bin/\:oc\-box\fR is the launcher. \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:oc\-box/\:\fR contains installed Python sources, documentation, LICENSE, NOTICE, and embedded \fBbuild\-info.json\fR\&. New installations stage complete versioned copies under \fBreleases/\:\fR and use a \fBcurrent\fR symlink plus compatibility pointers for the documented top\-level files. The launcher pins a release path so other active launchers keep their code unchanged. Old copies are retained, not automatically deleted. When first switching from an older flat installation, exit old active launchers before reinstalling; only launchers installed with release\-path pinning have this concurrent\-update guarantee. The source checkout and installation are independent; use \fBupgrade\fR or reinstall after updates. \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:man/\:man1/\:oc\-box.1\fR and \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:info/\:oc\-box.info\fR are the installed manuals. Installation requires no man/Info build tools.
.SS Host registry and archives
.PP
\fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box/\:workspaces/\:ID/\:workspace.json\fR holds directory identity, container/volume names, distro, limits, and saved Podman/device settings. \fBproject_identity\fR retains the last verified device/inode for diagnostics; \fBproject_filesystem_identity\fR, when supported, records \fBlinux\-file\-handle\-v1\fR filesystem ID and opaque persistent directory handle. Driver\-provided handles can include generation, dataset/subvolume, or other identifying information; oc\-box neither decodes them nor selects a format by filesystem name. The transient mount ID is not recorded. The registry need not share the project's filesystem: no hard links or filesystem\-specific utilities are used. \fBsessions.json\fR holds session IDs and archive associations. \fBimports/\:\fR holds private source exports; \fBhistory/\:\fR holds private host archive JSON files. \fBlock\fR serializes mutating commands. These files are outside the project and not mounted into the container. Transcript files may contain private content.
.PP
\fB\&.oc\-box\-id\fR in the project associates the directory with its registry. Do not edit or copy it to authorize a different mount. Identity checks include the canonical path, inode, and supported filesystem ID/persistent handle; older or unsupported filesystems use the conservative device/inode pair. A verified device\-only change does not by itself invalidate a stable directory baseline. Deleting/recreating the same path or losing its marker does not silently select the old environment or create a duplicate while an active registration remains. An explicitly requested \fBrepair\fR can accept the current identity at the same path and restore a missing marker, retaining the existing environment. With several registrations, select the intended workspace by ID; never copy markers between unrelated directories.
.PP
Plain purge retains verified archive recovery evidence under \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box/\:retired/\:ID/\:\fR, outside the active workspace registry. It contains only minimal ID/project provenance and private \fBhistory/\:\fR JSON files, not container storage, auth, installed dependencies, or the full live database. Keep it while those host archives may need importing; it is private saved data, not a project file or active environment. It has no automatic expiry.
.PP
The normal host OpenCode database, usually under \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:opencode/\:\fR, contains archive copies. Old installations may retain one\-time relocation backups under \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box/\:migrations/\:\fR; these are private data, not workspace resources or release artifacts, and later workspace purge leaves them intact. The original development\-only relocation helper is retired.
.SS Container data
.PP
The home volume is \fBoc\-box\-home\-ID\fR, mounted at the host user's home pathname. OpenCode data lives under its \fB\&.local/\:share/\:opencode/\:\fR\&. Copied configuration and generated \fBoc\-box\-profile.json\fR live under \fB\&.config/\:opencode/\:\fR\&. The matching OpenCode executable is \fB/\:opt/\:oc\-box/\:runtime/\:bin/\:opencode\fR; Node/npm, Python/uv, Bash, Git, ripgrep, curl, archive tools, and PDF tools are provided by baseline setup. Additional system tools belong to the container OS layer.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB0\fR means successful informational/lifecycle work or normal TUI exit. A TUI launch normally returns OpenCode/Podman's exit code. oc\-box validation, backend, setup, and sync failures return \fB1\fR; a successful TUI followed by failed history sync also returns \fB1\fR and retains container data. Argument syntax errors return \fB2\fR\&. A Python KeyboardInterrupt handled by the launcher returns \fB130\fR\&. An exception during final cleanup can also cause a nonzero result.
.PP
Warnings about host devices/SELinux do not themselves make creation fail. Podman/driver errors still propagate. A nonzero status is not permission to purge data; recover or inspect the existing environment first.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Everyday start and resume
.PP
.nf
.fam C
mkdir \-p ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-new
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The second launch resumes work. A new conversation does not reset files or installed tools. Exit OpenCode normally to synchronize history and stop it.
.SS Inspect from a second terminal
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
oc\-box \-\-status
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-json > status.json
podman ps \-a \-\-filter label=io.oc\-box.managed=true
.fam T
.fi
.SS Grow an existing environment without losing progress
.PP
Exit its client before reset. Preserve source, private home, and session IDs, but reinstall project\-specific system packages afterward:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-cpus 8 \-\-memory 16g \-\-pids\-limit 4096
.fam T
.fi
.SS Continue a host session and recover after a crash
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box sync \-\-project ~/devel/demo
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Import once; normal launches use the saved container session afterward.
.SS Replace persistent hardware settings
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/npu \-\-clear\-devices \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0 \e
  \-\-clear\-podman\-args \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-runtime=crun \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-group\-add=keep\-groups
.fam T
.fi
.SS Remove a disposable project completely
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/disposable \-\-all
oc\-box list
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If the directory was deleted manually, use its workspace ID instead:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
oc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-all
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The last command deletes its remaining managed resources and recorded host archives; an unrelated replacement directory will not be deleted.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS Reboot, remount, and directory identity changes
.PP
Clearing \fB~/\:.cache\fR does not erase the oc\-box registry (normally under \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box\fR) or private Podman volumes, and it does not change a directory's identity. Linux device numbers can change across reboot/remount; Btrfs commonly assigns a different \fBst_dev\fR while the directory inode stays the same. Those mount\-local numbers are not persistent disk identifiers.
.PP
The algorithm is capability\-based through Linux's common VFS interfaces, not Btrfs\-specific. It uses no ext4, XFS, Btrfs, bcachefs, or ZFS\-specific parser, ioctl, tool, or device path. On filesystems exposing \fBname_to_handle_at\fR, oc\-box combines the directory's opaque persistent handle with the \fBstatvfs\fR filesystem ID and inode. Drivers supply their own handle encoding; different formats and bounded variable sizes are accepted without a filesystem allowlist. If this stored identity matches and only the device number changed, launch/list/status accept the same directory automatically. No cache deletion, repair, reset, or session import is needed. Normal lifecycle launch updates the last verified device/inode while holding the workspace lock; read\-only listings do not write metadata. No extra command\-line tools, mount operations, or elevated capability are required for the handle lookup. The private registry and project may be on different filesystems, and a project's bind\-mount location need not be the filesystem root.
.PP
New workspaces record that baseline at creation. Matching old registrations are upgraded during the next normal start/sync/reset/terminal, or by explicit repair. If an old registry already differs after reboot and lacks the stored baseline, run one explicit repair after checking the directory; the launcher cannot safely infer the original filesystem from an old device number and matching inode. Keeping the same inode on a different filesystem is not enough.
.PP
If persistent handles are unsupported (including some overlay/network mounts), or the filesystem ID is unavailable/zero, the existing strict device/inode check remains. Driver/kernel support, not the filesystem's name, determines this behavior. oc\-box does not claim zero\-repair reboot compatibility with every possible filesystem driver. Once a strong baseline is recorded, an unavailable or different handle does not downgrade to a weaker check: it requires explicit repair. Filesystem/subvolume/snapshot restoration, inode/generation changes, copied markers, and mount replacements remain recovery decisions for the host operator. Older diagnostics incorrectly treated any changed device number as a replaced directory; do not remove the marker.
.PP
A filesystem\-independent logical ID would be just the canonical path plus a marker checked against the host registry. That works for selecting a workspace, but it cannot distinguish an exact copy/restore of that path and marker from the original directory. A signed marker has the same limitation because copies carry the signature too. oc\-box deliberately keeps physical replacement checks and explicit repair when the OS cannot provide enough evidence, rather than silently weakening the trust boundary on an unsupported driver. Clone/migration/restore operations are explicit repair cases, not ordinary reboot cases.
.PP
Keep the marker to retain the association with the existing environment. Update/reinstall the launcher, check that the project at the registered path is correct, and exit its active client. Then run from a host terminal:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box repair \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-resume
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If the container is still running, use \fBoc\-box stop \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID\fR and retry repair. Repair preserves the container, private volume, and session IDs. It does not recover storage that was deleted separately.
.PP
If the marker was already deleted, current launchers search the registry by canonical project path and print existing IDs/session information instead of creating a fresh environment. To restore a selected association:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-status
oc\-box repair \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID
oc\-box \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-resume
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If multiple records share the path, a path\-only repair does not guess. Choose the intended workspace by ID; another workspace's existing marker is not overwritten. If a replacement is already in use, retain it and remove the stale record with plain ID\-selected purge as described in Sessions and recovery.
.PP
If the marker has no matching host registry, check \fBOC_BOX_STATE_HOME\fR and \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR and restore the original registry before resuming. The default registry is \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box\fR, separate from \fB~/\:.cache\fR\&.
.SS Missing delegated CPU or memory controllers
.PP
On some openSUSE hosts a vendor user\-manager drop\-in clears delegation. Startup then fails on \fBmemory.swap.max\fR or missing cgroup controllers despite kernel support. The launcher reports this before starting the container. From a host terminal, substitute your numeric UID for 1000:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
sudo systemctl edit user@1000.service \-\-drop\-in=90\-oc\-box\-delegate.conf
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Save the override:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
[Service]
Delegate=cpu cpuset io memory pids
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Run \fBsudo systemctl daemon\-reload\fR\&. If the current user manager still lacks the controllers, fully end/restart it through a fresh login or reboot. A reboot is not a blanket Podman installation requirement. Check effective delegation in \fB/\:sys/\:fs/\:cgroup/\:user.slice/\:user\-1000.slice/\:user@1000.service/\:cgroup.controllers\fR\&. Retry the existing workspace without reset or purge. Do not edit vendor files.
.SS Project mount denied by SELinux
.PP
New mounts use Podman's shared \fB:z\fR project relabeling. Older mounts can exist but deny file access. Relabel only the project from the host:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
chcon \-R \-t container_file_t /path/to/project
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Resume normally; no session reimport/reset is needed. Device\-node labeling is separate: use the device\-policy diagnostics in Devices and host permissions.
.SS Slow first launch or blank terminal
.PP
Cold creation performs package and plugin downloads. Timed stage output and the temporary TUI loading line indicate progress. Warm starts retain compatible dependency state. Host SDK manifests older than runtime are normalized inside the copied config, not by modifying host configuration. Preserve the environment when diagnosing a wait; reset discards the OS cache and can repeat cold setup.
.SS Active workspace lock or failed import
.PP
Exit the active launcher before start/sync/reset/repair/terminal/purge. Status and stop remain available in another terminal. Do not delete the lock file to bypass a live process. For a failed import, retry the same flag with an updated launcher; recovery uses the saved export without overwriting existing container work. For a failed archive sync, use \fBsync\fR; the original live DB remains in its volume. A deleted private volume cannot be recovered from session metadata alone.
.SS Maintenance shell environment
.PP
Use \fBoc\-box terminal\fR to debug an existing container without launching OpenCode or supplying host tokens. A missing command may need installing in that container; terminal does not install baseline tools automatically. Its shell deliberately skips user profiles, shell history, and normal credential forwarding. A missing project marker or changed identity still requires explicit \fBrepair\fR; terminal cannot bypass those checks. If it reports an active launcher, exit OpenCode and wait for its history sync/stop before retrying. Do not remove the workspace lock file to bypass the running process.
.SH INSTALLATION AND MANUALS
.PP
Requires Linux, Python 3.10 or later, rootless Podman with pasta networking, delegated cgroup CPU/memory/PIDs controllers, and host OpenCode 1.x. Session import/export compatibility was checked with OpenCode 1.19.5. Other major OpenCode versions are rejected rather than assuming database/CLI compatibility. Podman must work from the regular host user, not through sudo. Network access is needed for initial distribution, runtime, and dependency downloads.
.SS Python package installations
.PP
Selected releases are available at https://pypi.org/project/oc\-box/. Choose one package\-managed CLI installation, not system pip:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
pipx install oc\-box
pipx ensurepath
# Or:
uv tool install oc\-box
uv tool update\-shell
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Open a new terminal if those commands add the tool\-manager bin directory to PATH. For standard pip, create and activate a dedicated virtual environment:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
python3 \-m venv ~/.venvs/oc\-box
\&. ~/.venvs/oc\-box/bin/activate
python \-m pip install oc\-box
oc\-box \-\-version
.fam T
.fi
.PP
On later visits, activate it again or call \fB~/\:.venvs/\:oc\-box/\:bin/\:oc\-box\fR directly.
.PP
Podman, OpenCode, Linux/cgroup configuration, and device drivers are external host requirements, not Python dependencies. Package installation never creates the curl installer's \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:oc\-box\fR release tree or a competing global wrapper. pipx/uv expose the package's console entrypoint in their normal bin directory. \fBpython \-m oc_box\fR is also available inside the package environment.
.PP
Upgrade through the owner of the installation:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
pipx upgrade oc\-box
uv tool upgrade oc\-box
# Inside the installed virtual environment:
python \-m pip install \-\-upgrade oc\-box
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The regular GitHub/curl installer and updater remain supported. Both channels read the same workspace registry, projects, and private containers. If several launchers exist, use \fBcommand \-v oc\-box\fR to check which PATH entry is selected; avoid keeping conflicting launchers in the same bin location. Source commits continue to publish on GitHub independently of selected PyPI releases.
.PP
Wheels include runtime scripts, prebuilt manuals, license/notice, and source metadata in the isolated \fBoc_box\fR namespace. Manuals also install under the Python environment's \fBshare/\:man/\:man1\fR and \fBshare/\:info\fR directories; tool\-manager environments are not necessarily in the host readers' default search path. For example, after activating the relevant environment:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
man "$VIRTUAL_ENV/share/man/man1/oc\-box.1"
info \-\-file "$VIRTUAL_ENV/share/info/oc\-box.info"
.fam T
.fi
.PP
No source checkout, setuptools, documentation build tools, or third\-party Python runtime dependency is required to use an installed wheel. For building or publishing, see CONTRIBUTING.md: GitHub's \fBpublish.yml\fR workflow uses the \fBpypi\fR environment and PyPI Trusted Publishing without long\-lived upload tokens.
.SS GitHub/curl installations
.PP
One\-line installation from the public GitHub branch requires curl, Git, and Python 3.10+ already available on the host. It installs oc\-box, not Podman or host OpenCode, and executes the downloaded installer as your user:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
curl \-fsSL \-\-proto '=https' \-\-tlsv1.2 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/groopcd/oc\-box/master/install.sh | sh
.fam T
.fi
.PP
To inspect before execution instead of piping to a shell:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
curl \-fsSL \-\-proto '=https' \-\-tlsv1.2 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/groopcd/oc\-box/master/install.sh \-o install\-oc\-box.sh
# Review install\-oc\-box.sh, then:
sh install\-oc\-box.sh
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The bootstrap clones full history anonymously into a private temporary directory and removes that temporary checkout when finished. It does not configure Git authentication, download credentials, or keep a source checkout in your project. When moving from an older flat installation, exit its active oc\-box launchers first; future versioned installations can coexist with already running versions.
.PP
Alternatively install from the source checkout as your host user:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
python3 install.py
oc\-box \-\-version
man oc\-box
info oc\-box
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The installer copies prebuilt manual files and does not require makeinfo, groff, man, or info. Reading them needs the host reader; on openSUSE those are the \fBman\fR and \fBinfo\fR packages. It updates only the user's Info index when an \fBinstall\-info\fR utility is available, not any system index. Ensure \fB~/\:.local/\:bin\fR is on PATH. If local documentation directories are not searched:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
MANPATH="$HOME/.local/share/man:" man oc\-box
INFOPATH="$HOME/.local/share/info:" info oc\-box
man "$HOME/.local/share/man/man1/oc\-box.1"
info \-\-file "$HOME/.local/share/info/oc\-box.info"
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The full CLI reference is \fBdocs/\:cli.md\fR; the source tree includes generated \fBdocs/\:oc\-box.1\fR, \fBdocs/\:oc\-box.texi\fR, and \fBdocs/\:oc\-box.info\fR\&. Maintainers regenerate them with \fBpython3 tools/\:build_docs.py\fR; that build requires GNU makeinfo. \fBpython3 tools/\:build_docs.py \-\-check\fR checks the generated man/Texinfo source. End users do not need to rebuild documentation to install it.
